TEXT -544 -192 Left 6 ;The generator is switched to the isolated network\nAfter 6.5s the bus is shorted at the generator terminal before the transformer\nThe maximum short circuit current at start of fault is\nvery high and the steady state current is much lower\nHINT: If the speed controller is not limited \nthe short circuit current is only a little bit higher- > test it\nQuestion: Can the frequency be stabilized if speed controller\nis not limited -> test it without limit and longer simulation \ntime intervall (20s)\nThe maximal short circuit current is limited mainly by setting streuung (leakage) to a value about 0.05\nRcu influence only the decrease of the current (time constant)\nYou can see that the starting current is about 10 times higher as the steady state current\nif the generator is shorted directly at the generator terminals (shorted current is a function of leakage)\nThe generator short circuit current depends also of the timepoint of shorting\nPhase 1: Voltage is zero at shorting: shorted current is highest and dc-offset ist maximal\nPhase 2,3: Voltage is high/low at shorting: I is smaller and smaller dc-offset is present\nNOTE: The value of  the magnetic field current IE = ix(U1:F1) is high at shorting time \nThe time constant of high valued IE is very low (because of high RE value=Ue0/Ie0) ->\nTest Time constant by setting UE0 to 100V instead of 600V (Synchronous machine parameter)\nNOTE: You have to set also external controll voltage to "verr verr 0 100"\nThe dc-value of IE is overlapped by a transient ac current\nThe short circuit current is mainly ohmic (see phase angle) and so the load angle is 90 degrees\n(output of mainly active power)
TEXT -992 312 Left 2 ;After 5s the speed controller \nis limited
